#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <chrono>
#include <atomic>

class SemaphoreManager {
public:
    SemaphoreManager(int maxSemaphores) : maxSemaphores(maxSemaphores), semaphoreCount(0) {}

    void acquire() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        condition.wait(lock, [this] { return semaphoreCount > 0; });
        semaphoreCount--;
    }

    void release() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        if (semaphoreCount < maxSemaphores) {
            semaphoreCount++;
            condition.notify_one();
        }
    }

private:
    int maxSemaphores;
    int semaphoreCount;
    std::mutex mutex;
    std::condition_variable condition;
};

int main() {
    SemaphoreManager manager(100);
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < 150; ++i) {
        threads.emplace_back([&manager]() {
            manager.acquire();
            // Simulate work
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(10));
            manager.release();
        });
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code snippet provides a basic implementation of a semaphore manager in C++. It includes methods to acquire and release semaphores, ensuring that only the specified number of concurrent operations are allowed. The system is designed to be thread-safe using mutexes and condition variables, and it can handle up to a limited number of simultaneously active semaphores.

For a more comprehensive solution, you would need to integrate with an external cloud-based API for semaphore lifecycle management, implement load balancing and auto-scaling strategies, add a monitoring and logging framework, develop self-healing mechanisms, optimize resource utilization, incorporate predictive analytics, and design a web-based dashboard. This involves complex systems engineering and DevOps practices that go beyond the scope of this code snippet.

This example is intended to illustrate the basic principles of semaphore management in C++. For production use, you would need to consider additional factors such as error handling, security, and scalability optimizations.']

