#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>

// Event structure
struct Event {
    std::string timestamp;
    std::string eventType;
    void* metadata; // Pointer to event-specific data
};

// Event processor thread function
void processEvents(std::queue<Event>& eventQueue, std::mutex& queueMutex, std::condition_variable& queueCond) {
    while (true) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(queueMutex);
        queueCond.wait(lock, []{ return !eventQueue.empty() || !running; });

        if (!running) break;

        Event event = eventQueue.front();
        eventQueue.pop();
        lock.unlock();

        // Process the event (e.g., log to legacy system, send to analytics, publish on message broker)
        processEvent(event);
    }
}

// Main function
int main() {
    std::queue<Event> eventQueue;
    std::mutex queueMutex;
    std::condition_variable queueCond;
    std::vector<std::thread> processors;
    std::atomic<bool> running(true);

    // Initialize processor threads
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numProcessors; ++i) {
        processors.emplace_back(processEvents, std::ref(eventQueue), std::ref(queueMutex), std::ref(queueCond));
    }

    // Read events from file and enqueue them
    std::ifstream inputFile("events.txt");
    if (inputFile.is_open()) {
        while (!inputFile.eof()) {
            Event event;
            inputFile >> event.timestamp >> event.eventType;
            event.metadata = new void*; // Example metadata allocation
            eventQueue.push(event);
            queueCond.notify_one();
        }
        inputFile.close();
    }

    // Join processor threads
    for (auto& t : processors) {
        t.join();
    }

    // Cleanup
    running = false;
    queueCond.notify_all();
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numProcessors; ++i) {
        processors[i].join();
    }
    for (Event& event : eventQueue) {
        delete static_cast<void*>(event.metadata);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code snippet provides a basic structure for an event processing framework in C++. It includes a main function that reads events from a file, enqueues them into a shared queue, and spawns multiple threads to process these events. The `processEvents` function is responsible for dequeuing events and processing them (e.g., logging, analytics, message broker integration).

Please note that this code is highly simplified and lacks many features such as error handling, performance optimization strategies, health monitoring, and integration with external systems like legacy logging, third-party analytics, and distributed message brokers. It also does not include container orchestration or predictive load balancing.

To fully implement a high-throughput, resilient event processing framework, you would need to expand upon this basic structure by incorporating more advanced features, error handling mechanisms, performance optimization techniques, and integrations with external systems and tools. Additionally, consider using a more sophisticated data structure for the event queue if performance becomes an issue at higher loads.']

